/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"bassstation-level-2","number":2,"title":"Comprehensive Physical Hardware Map","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Identify and physically locate all twelve functional sections across the Bass Station II top control surface. - Understand the digital parameter pickup and display behavior governed by the 3-character 7-segment LED screen and the dual Ori","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-2-1","code":"2","title":"Comprehensive Physical Hardware Map","blurb":"- Identify and physically locate all twelve functional sections across the Bass Station II top control surface. - Understand the digital parameter pickup and display behavior governed by the 3-character 7-segment LED screen and the dual Ori","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o-2","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 2.1 — Top Panel Tour & Navigation Controls","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Identify and physically locate all twelve functional sections across the Bass Station II top control surface.","Understand the digital parameter pickup and display behavior governed by the 3-character 7-segment LED screen and the dual Original Value indicator LEDs.","Explain the multi-function architecture of shared physical controls (Oscillator 1/2, LFO 1/2, Mixer Sources, Envelopes Amp/Mod).","Delineate the tactile operation of the performance controls (Pitch Wheel, Mod Wheel, Octave Shift, Transpose, Function/Exit)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-1","title":"2.1.1: Master Section Architecture and Display Logic","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Master Section is located in the upper-left quadrant of the front panel and houses the primary global controls and visual feedback indicators."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  MASTER SECTION TOPOLOGY\n    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n    │  [VOLUME]       ┌─────────┐      [ ◄ ] [ ► ]           │\n    │  (Analog Pot)   │  [8.8.8]│      (Original Value LEDs) │\n    │                 └─────────┘                            │\n    │               [PATCH / VALUE]        [SAVE]            │\n    │                 [▲]   [▼]         (Tactile Switch)     │\n    └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Volume Potentiometer</strong>:","A continuous rotary potentiometer directly controlling the attenuation of the final analogue output amplifier stage (driving both Line Out and Headphones).","This control is 100% analog; it is not scanned digitally and does not transmit or receive MIDI Volume (CC 7) messages.","<strong>Three-Character 7-Segment Alphanumeric LED Display</strong>:","Displays the current active patch number (<code>0</code> to <code>127</code>).","When a physical parameter is moved, the display temporarily switches to show the real-time parameter value (<code>0</code> to <code>127</code>, bipolar <code>-63</code> to <code>+63</code>, or alphanumeric mode flags such as <code>LP</code>, <code>BP</code>, <code>HP</code>, <code>Snc</code>, <code>SPd</code>).","If no controls are manipulated for approximately 1.5 seconds, the display automatically reverts to the current patch number.","<strong>Original Value Indicator LEDs (<code>◄</code> and <code>►</code>)</strong>:","Assist in sound design by indicating the relationship between the physical position of a potentiometer/fader and the stored value in the digital patch memory:","<strong>Left LED (<code>◄</code>) illuminated</strong>: Physical control position is <em>lower</em> than the stored patch value. Rotate clockwise to match.","<strong>Right LED (<code>►</code>) illuminated</strong>: Physical control position is <em>higher</em> than the stored patch value. Rotate anti-clockwise to match.","<strong>Both LEDs illuminated (<code>◄ ►</code>)</strong>: Physical control position <em>matches</em> the exact value stored in memory.","<strong>Patch / Value Buttons (<code>▲</code> and <code>▼</code>)</strong>:","Increment or decrement the active patch number.","When in On-Key editing mode or parameter submenu mode, these buttons adjust parameter values in discrete single-unit steps (holding down advances rapidly).","<strong>Save Button</strong>:","Initiates the patch writing routine into non-volatile memory. Pressing <strong>Save</strong> causes the current patch number on the LED display to flash."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Patch <code>0</code> using the <strong>Patch ▼</strong> button.","Turn the <strong>Filter Frequency</strong> knob slowly from fully counter-clockwise to fully clockwise.","Observe:","The display changes from <code>0</code> to numeric values (<code>0</code> through <code>127</code>).","The left LED (<code>◄</code>) illuminates when below the stored value, both LEDs (<code>◄ ►</code>) illuminate at the stored threshold, and the right LED (<code>►</code>) illuminates above it.","Stop rotating the knob and wait 2 seconds; confirm the display automatically reverts to <code>0</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What do simultaneous illuminated <code>◄</code> and <code>►</code> Original Value LEDs indicate?","[ ] How long does the display hold a parameter value before returning to the active patch number?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-2","title":"2.1.2: Oscillator Section Controls and Routing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Oscillator section utilizes a shared control surface to configure two identical primary analogue oscillators (Osc 1 and Osc 2) and an integrated Sub-Oscillator."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">               OSCILLATOR SECTION CONTROL MAP\n ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n │ [Osc Select]    [Waveform]     [Range]                      │\n │ (1 / 2 Switch)  (Sin/Tri/Saw/Pul) (16'/8'/4'/2')            │\n │                                                             │\n │ [Coarse]        [Fine]         [Mod Env Depth] [LFO 1 Depth]│\n │ (±1 Octave)     (±100 Cents)   (Pitch Envelope)(Pitch Vibrato)│\n │                                                             │\n │ [Pulse Width]   [PWM Source]   [Sub Octave]    [Sub Wave]   │\n │ (Potentiometer) (Man/Mod/LFO2) (-1 / -2)       (Sin/Pul/Sqr)│\n │                                                             │\n │ [SYNC LED] (Osc 1-2 Hard Sync Active Indicator)             │\n └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Osc Select Switch (1 / 2)</strong>:","Determines which primary oscillator is currently mapped to the physical controls.","Toggling between <code>1</code> and <code>2</code> does not reset or alter the parameters of the unselected oscillator; all parameters remain intact in digital memory.","<strong>Waveform Select Button &amp; LEDs</strong>:","Cycles through four discrete analog waveform topologies: <strong>Sine</strong>, <strong>Triangle</strong>, <strong>Sawtooth</strong>, and <strong>Pulse / Square</strong>.","<strong>Range Rotary Switch</strong>:","Selects the base pitch octave using traditional organ pipe-length designations: <strong>16'</strong> (sub-bass), <strong>8'</strong> (standard concert pitch where Middle C corresponds to C3), <strong>4'</strong> (one octave up), and <strong>2'</strong> (two octaves up).","<strong>Coarse &amp; Fine Tuning Potentiometers</strong>:","<strong>Coarse</strong>: Sweeps pitch by ±1 octave (±12 semitones) in continuous increments.","<strong>Fine</strong>: Finely offsets pitch by ±100 cents (±1 semitone) for acoustic chorus/beating effects.","<strong>Pitch Modulation Depths (Mod Env &amp; LFO 1)</strong>:","<strong>Mod Env</strong>: Center-zero bipolar rotary pot. Clockwise sweeps pitch upwards during envelope attack; counter-clockwise sweeps pitch downwards.","<strong>LFO 1</strong>: Bipolar rotary pot controlling pitch modulation (vibrato or audio-rate FM effects).","<strong>Pulse Width &amp; Source Controls</strong>:","Active only when the <strong>Pulse</strong> waveform is selected.","<strong>Source Switch</strong>: Selects modulation source: <strong>Manual</strong> (pot sets static duty cycle ~5% to 95%), <strong>Mod Env</strong> (envelope shapes duty cycle), or <strong>LFO 2</strong> (LFO 2 provides continuous pulse-width modulation).","<strong>Sub-Oscillator Dedicated Switches</strong>:","<strong>Sub Octave</strong>: <code>-1</code> (one octave below Osc 1) or <code>-2</code> (two octaves below Osc 1).","<strong>Sub Wave</strong>: <strong>Sine</strong>, <strong>Narrow Pulse</strong>, or <strong>Square</strong>.","<strong>Sync LED</strong>:","Illuminates when Oscillator 1–2 Hard Synchronization is active via On-Key setting."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set <strong>Osc Select</strong> to <code>1</code>. Select the <strong>Sawtooth</strong> waveform and <strong>8'</strong> Range.","Flip <strong>Osc Select</strong> to <code>2</code>. Select the <strong>Sine</strong> waveform and <strong>4'</strong> Range.","Switch back to <code>1</code>. Confirm that the Waveform LED for Osc 1 remains on Sawtooth and Range remains at 8'."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What are the four available primary waveforms in the Oscillator section?","[ ] What parameter determines whether the Sub-Oscillator tracks one or two octaves below Oscillator 1?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-3","title":"2.1.3: LFO Section Controls","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The LFO (Low Frequency Oscillator) section contains controls for two independent modulation generators: <strong>LFO 1</strong> (primarily routed to pitch and pulse width) and <strong>LFO 2</strong> (primarily routed to filter cutoff and pulse width)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  LFO SECTION TOPOLOGY\n    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n    │  [LFO 1 / 2 Select]    [Delay / Speed Select]          │\n    │  (Toggle Switch)       (Toggle Switch)                 │\n    │                                                        │\n    │  [Waveform Select]     (Triangle / Saw / Sqr / S&amp;H)    │\n    │  (Button + LEDs)                                       │\n    │                                                        │\n    │  [LFO 1 Rotary Pot]    [LFO 2 Rotary Pot]              │\n    │  (Sets LFO 1 Rate/Dly) (Sets LFO 2 Rate/Dly)           │\n    └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Dual Independent Rotary Potentiometers</strong>:","Unlike the oscillator section, the LFO section provides <strong>two dedicated rotary pots</strong> side-by-side for LFO 1 and LFO 2.","<strong>Delay / Speed Switch</strong>:","Determines the operational function of the two rotary potentiometers:","<strong>Speed</strong>: Adjusts LFO frequency (rate) from 0 Hz up to ~190 Hz in free-running mode, or selects sync divisions in clock-synced mode.","<strong>Delay</strong>: Sets an initial fade-in onset time (0 to several seconds) before LFO modulation reaches full amplitude after a key is struck.","<strong>Waveform Select Button &amp; LEDs</strong>:","Selects the active shape for the currently selected LFO: <strong>Triangle</strong>, <strong>Sawtooth (falling)</strong>, <strong>Square</strong>, or <strong>Sample &amp; Hold (random)</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set the <strong>Delay/Speed</strong> switch to <strong>Speed</strong>.","Turn the <strong>LFO 1</strong> knob to minimum (zero); note the slow modulation cycle.","Turn the <strong>LFO 1</strong> knob to maximum; note the high-speed audio-rate modulation (~190 Hz).","Set the <strong>Delay/Speed</strong> switch to <strong>Delay</strong>. Rotate the <strong>LFO 1</strong> knob to maximum. Strike a key and observe that modulation is initially absent, gradually fading in over several seconds."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Which four waveforms can be generated by LFO 1 and LFO 2?","[ ] What is the function of the Delay parameter in the LFO architecture?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-4","title":"2.1.4: Mixer Section Controls and Source Routing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Mixer section combines six discrete audio sources into a single monophonic summing bus feeding the analogue filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  MIXER SECTION TOPOLOGY\n    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n    │  [OSC 1 Level]   [OSC 2 Level]   [SUB Level]           │\n    │  (Dedicated Pot) (Dedicated Pot) (Dedicated Pot)       │\n    │                                                        │\n    │  [Noise / Ring / Ext Level]      [Source Switch]       │\n    │  (Shared Potentiometer)          (Noise / Ring / Ext)  │\n    └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Dedicated Source Potentiometers</strong>:","<strong>Osc 1</strong>: Continuous level control (0–127) for Oscillator 1.","<strong>Osc 2</strong>: Continuous level control (0–127) for Oscillator 2.","<strong>Sub</strong>: Continuous level control (0–127) for the Sub-Oscillator.","<strong>Shared Source Potentiometer &amp; Assign Switch</strong>:","A single fourth potentiometer adjusts the level for <strong>Noise</strong>, <strong>Ring Modulation</strong>, or <strong>External Input</strong>.","The three-position slide switch (<strong>Noise / Ring / Ext</strong>) assigns the pot to the selected source.","<em>Memory Retention</em>: Adjusting the pot for Noise, switching to Ring, and turning the pot adjusts Ring <em>without changing the previously set Noise level</em>. Each of the three shared sources maintains its independent level in digital memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On an initialized patch, set <strong>Osc 1</strong>, <strong>Osc 2</strong>, and <strong>Sub</strong> pots fully counter-clockwise (zero).","Set the shared switch to <strong>Noise</strong> and turn the shared pot to maximum. Confirm white noise is audible.","Set the shared switch to <strong>Ring</strong> and turn the shared pot to zero. Confirm silence.","Flip the switch back to <strong>Noise</strong>. Strike a key; confirm the Noise source is still at maximum volume."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] How many total audio sources can be simultaneously mixed into the filter stage?","[ ] When switching the shared mixer control from Noise to Ring, is the Noise level reset to zero?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-5","title":"2.1.5: Filter Section Controls and Topologies","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Filter section shapes harmonic frequency distribution using discrete analogue filter circuits."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                   FILTER SECTION TOPOLOGY\n    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n    │  [TYPE] (Classic / Acid)     [SHAPE] (LP / BP / HP)    │\n    │  [SLOPE] (12dB / 24dB)       [OVERDRIVE] (Pre-Filter)  │\n    │                                                        │\n    │  ┌──────────────┐                                      │\n    │  │  FREQUENCY   │            [RESONANCE]               │\n    │  │ (Large Dial) │            (Peak Emphasis Pot)       │\n    │  └──────────────┘                                      │\n    │                                                        │\n    │  [Mod Env Depth]             [LFO 2 Depth]             │\n    │  (Bipolar Cutoff Modulation) (Bipolar Cutoff Mod)      │\n    └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Type Select Switch (Classic / Acid)</strong>:","<strong>Classic</strong>: State-variable filter with selectable shape and slope.","<strong>Acid</strong>: 4-pole (24 dB/octave) diode-ladder low-pass filter with characteristic non-linear resonance saturation.","<strong>Shape &amp; Slope Switches (Classic Mode Only)</strong>:","<strong>Shape</strong>: Low Pass (<strong>LP</strong>), Band Pass (<strong>BP</strong>), High Pass (<strong>HP</strong>).","<strong>Slope</strong>: <strong>12 dB</strong> (2-pole, gentle cutoff slope) or <strong>24 dB</strong> (4-pole, steep cutoff slope).","<em>Note</em>: When <strong>Acid</strong> is selected, Shape and Slope switches are completely bypassed and inoperative (Acid is permanently 24 dB Low-Pass).","<strong>Frequency Rotary Potentiometer</strong>:","Oversized, high-precision dial controlling filter cutoff frequency (or center frequency in Band Pass mode).","<strong>Resonance Potentiometer</strong>:","Boosts signal amplitude at the cutoff frequency. High settings drive the circuit into self-oscillation.","<strong>Overdrive Potentiometer</strong>:","Pre-filter analog gain stage introducing warm saturation and harmonic distortion directly into the filter input.","<strong>Modulation Depth Controls</strong>:","<strong>Mod Env</strong>: Bipolar potentiometer routing the Modulation Envelope to filter cutoff.","<strong>LFO 2</strong>: Bipolar potentiometer routing LFO 2 to filter cutoff."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select the <strong>Classic</strong> filter type. Set Shape to <strong>LP</strong> and Slope to <strong>24 dB</strong>.","Sweep the <strong>Frequency</strong> knob; observe standard low-pass filtering.","Switch Type to <strong>Acid</strong>. Observe the immediate shift in resonance character and harmonic bite.","Toggle Shape between LP, BP, and HP while in <strong>Acid</strong> mode; confirm that the audio output remains strictly Low-Pass regardless of the Shape switch position."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What happens to the Slope and Shape controls when the Filter Type switch is set to Acid?","[ ] Is the Overdrive circuit placed before or after the filter section?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-6","title":"2.1.6: Envelopes Section Controls","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Envelopes section features four high-precision 30 mm tactile faders operating as four-stage ADSR (Attack, Decay, Sustain, Release) envelope generators."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  ENVELOPES SECTION TOPOLOGY\n    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n    │  [Env Select]              [Triggering]                │\n    │  (Amp / Mod / Both)        (Autoglide / Single / Multi)│\n    │                                                        │\n    │  ┌───┐         ┌───┐          ┌───┐          ┌───┐     │\n    │  │ A │         │ D │          │ S │          │ R │     │\n    │  │ T │         │ E │          │ U │          │ E │     │\n    │  │ T │         │ C │          │ S │          │ L │     │\n    │  └───┘         └───┘          └───┘          └───┘     │\n    │  (Attack Fader)(Decay Fader) (Sustain Fader)(Rel Fader)│\n    └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Env Select Switch</strong>:","<strong>Amp</strong>: Faders adjust the Amplitude Envelope (hardwired to the VCA).","<strong>Mod</strong>: Faders adjust the Modulation Envelope (routed to Pitch, Pulse Width, and Filter Cutoff).","<strong>Both (Amp + Mod)</strong>: Faders adjust both envelope generators simultaneously, linking their ADSR curves.","<strong>ADSR Faders</strong>:","<strong>Attack</strong>: Sets the time taken for the envelope to reach maximum amplitude upon note-on (0 ms to ~several seconds; mid-position ≈ 250 ms).","<strong>Decay</strong>: Sets the time taken to drop from peak level to the Sustain level (mid-position ≈ 150 ms).","<strong>Sustain</strong>: Sets the steady-state amplitude level maintained while the key is held (0 to 100%).","<strong>Release</strong>: Sets the time taken to return to zero amplitude after key release (mid-position ≈ 360 ms; maximum &gt; 10 s).","<strong>Triggering Switch</strong>:","<strong>Single</strong>: Envelopes do not re-trigger if new notes are played legato (overlapping).","<strong>Multi</strong>: Envelopes re-trigger on every keypress, regardless of legato overlap.","<strong>Autoglide</strong>: Envelopes behave as Single trigger, and Portamento (glide) activates only on legato note transitions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set <strong>Env Select</strong> to <strong>Amp</strong>. Set Attack to 0, Decay to 0, Sustain to maximum, and Release to 0 (instant organ-style gating).","Set <strong>Env Select</strong> to <strong>Mod</strong>. Set Attack to 50%, Decay to 50%, Sustain to 0, and Release to 0.","Increase <strong>Mod Env</strong> depth in the Filter section to maximum.","Strike a key and confirm that the audio opens and closes dynamically via the Mod Envelope while the overall note volume maintains steady sustain via the Amp Envelope."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What occurs when the Env Select switch is set to the center position (Amp + Mod)?","[ ] What is the operational difference between Single and Multi triggering modes?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-7","title":"2.1.7: Arpeggiator and Sequencer Shared Control Panel","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Arpeggiator and 32-Step Sequencer share a unified set of rotary controls and buttons in the upper-right section of the top panel."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">            ARP / SEQUENCER SHARED PANEL TOPOLOGY\n ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n │ [On / Legato]      [Latch / Rest]                           │\n │ (Arp On / Seq Tie) (Arp Latch / Seq Rest / Key Hold)        │\n │                                                             │\n │ [Tempo]            [Rhythm]         [Mode]         [Oct/Seq]│\n │ (40–240 BPM)       (32 Patterns)    (Arp Modes)    (1–4 Oct)│\n │                                     (Record/Play)  (SEQ 1–4)│\n └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Dual Silkscreen Legends</strong>:","<strong>Standard White Text</strong>: Refers to real-time Arpeggiator functions (<strong>On</strong>, <strong>Latch</strong>, <strong>Tempo</strong>, <strong>Rhythm</strong>, <strong>Mode</strong>, <strong>Octaves</strong>).","<strong>Black-on-White Inverted Text</strong>: Refers to Sequencer functions (<strong>Legato</strong>, <strong>Rest</strong>, <strong>Record</strong>, <strong>Play</strong>, <strong>SEQ 1–4</strong>).","<strong>Control Mapping</strong>:","<strong>Tempo</strong>: Adjusts internal clock rate from 40 to 240 BPM.","<strong>Rhythm</strong>: Selects one of 32 pre-programmed rhythmic velocity/timing patterns.","<strong>Mode Rotary</strong>:","<em>Arpeggiator Modes</em>: <strong>Up</strong>, <strong>Down</strong>, <strong>UpDn</strong>, <strong>UpDn2</strong>, <strong>Played</strong>, <strong>Random</strong>.","<em>Sequencer Modes</em>: <strong>Record</strong> (enters sequence programming mode), <strong>Play</strong> (playback active sequence).","<strong>Octaves / SEQ Control</strong>:","<em>Arp Mode</em>: Sets arpeggio span across <strong>1</strong>, <strong>2</strong>, <strong>3</strong>, or <strong>4</strong> octaves.","<em>Seq Mode</em>: Selects one of the four non-volatile sequence memory banks (<strong>SEQ 1</strong>, <strong>SEQ 2</strong>, <strong>SEQ 3</strong>, <strong>SEQ 4</strong>).","<strong>Buttons</strong>:","<strong>On / Legato</strong>: Enables Arpeggiator when in normal play; inserts a tied/legato step when in Sequencer Record mode.","<strong>Latch / Rest</strong>: Latches arpeggiator patterns; enables Key Hold when Arp is Off; inserts a silent step when in Sequencer Record mode."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Press the <strong>On</strong> button in the Arp section (LED illuminates).","Hold a three-note chord. Rotate the <strong>Mode</strong> switch between <strong>Up</strong>, <strong>Down</strong>, and <strong>Random</strong> to verify pattern changes.","Turn the <strong>Mode</strong> switch to <strong>Record</strong>. Observe the LED display changes to <code>rec</code> and the step counter begins at step <code>1</code>.","Turn the <strong>Mode</strong> switch back to <strong>Up</strong> to return to arpeggiator operation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Which two positions on the Mode rotary switch activate the Step Sequencer?","[ ] What is the secondary function of the Latch button when the Arpeggiator is powered off?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-8","title":"2.1.8: Portamento and Analogue Effects Controls","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Located directly below the Filter section, the Portamento and Effects section provides continuous analogue coloration and glide control."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">               PORTAMENTO &amp; EFFECTS TOPOLOGY\n ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n │  [GLIDE TIME]         [DISTORTION]       [OSC FILTER MOD]   │\n │  (Portamento Rate)    (Post-Filter Drive)(FM: Osc 2→Filter) │\n └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Glide Time (Portamento)</strong>:","Sets the rate at which pitch slides between consecutive notes.","Fully counter-clockwise disables portamento (instantaneous pitch jump). Rotating clockwise lengthens glide time up to several seconds.","<strong>Distortion</strong>:","Adds discrete post-filter analogue clipping before the VCA stage.","Because it is placed pre-VCA, the harmonic distortion character remains constant regardless of decaying volume envelopes.","<strong>Osc Filter Mod (Oscillator 2 Filter Modulation)</strong>:","Direct audio-rate frequency modulation (FM) of the filter cutoff frequency driven by the output waveform of <strong>Oscillator 2</strong>.","Produces complex bell-like, metallic, metallic-growl, and sideband-rich acoustic textures."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set <strong>Glide Time</strong> to the 12 o'clock position. Play two alternating keys an octave apart; observe smooth pitch transitions.","Turn <strong>Distortion</strong> to 50%; listen to the aggressive saturation introduced to the waveform.","Return Distortion to zero and turn <strong>Osc Filter Mod</strong> to 75%. Adjust the <strong>Fine</strong> tuning of <strong>Oscillator 2</strong> and hear complex harmonic sidebands generated by filter cross-modulation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Where is the Distortion circuit located in relation to the Filter and the VCA?","[ ] Which oscillator supplies the modulation signal for the Osc Filter Mod control?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-9","title":"2.1.9: Left-Hand Performance Controls and Function Interface","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The left cheek and performance area houses mechanical modulation controllers and system entry keys."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">               PERFORMANCE CONTROLLERS TOPOLOGY\n ┌────────────────────────┐  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n │       ┌───────┐        │  │  [OCTAVE ▲]     [TRANSPOSE]     │\n │       │ MOD   │        │  │  [OCTAVE ▼]                     │\n │       │ WHEEL │        │  │                                 │\n │       │ (Ill) │        │  │  [FUNCTION / EXIT]              │\n │       └───────┘        │  │  (System / On-Key Edit Access)  │\n │       ┌───────┐        │  │                                 │\n │       │ PITCH │        │  └─────────────────────────────────┘\n │       │ WHEEL │        │\n │       │(Spring│        │\n │       └───────┘        │\n └────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pitch Wheel</strong>:","Center-sprung mechanical wheel for pitch bending. Automatically snaps back to zero upon release. Default bend range is ±12 semitones (user-adjustable from -24 to +24 semitones via On-Key).","<strong>Modulation Wheel</strong>:","Friction-mounted, continuously illuminated rotary wheel (retains position when released). Transmits standard MIDI CC 1 and routes to user-assigned destinations (Filter Cutoff, LFO depths, Osc 2 Pitch).","<strong>Octave Shift Buttons (<code>▲</code> and <code>▼</code>)</strong>:","Transposes the 25-note physical keybed across a range of <strong>−5 to +4 octaves</strong>.","Pressing both <strong>Octave ▲ and ▼ simultaneously</strong> instantly resets the keyboard to default octave (<code>0</code>).","<strong>Transpose Button</strong>:","Hold <strong>Transpose</strong> and press any key on the keyboard to transpose global pitch relative to Middle C in semitone increments. Holding <strong>Transpose</strong> and pressing Middle C resets transposition.","<strong>Function / Exit Button</strong>:","Puts the synthesizer into <strong>On-Key configuration mode</strong>, enabling secondary parameter editing printed across the 25 keybed notes. Pressing <strong>Function</strong> again exits and saves parameters to the active edit buffer."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Shift the keybed down by pressing <strong>Octave ▼</strong> twice. Observe the LED display momentarily confirm <code>-2</code>.","Press both <strong>Octave ▲ and ▼</strong> simultaneously. Observe the display confirm <code>0</code> (reset).","Press the <strong>Function / Exit</strong> button; note that the LED display shows the current value of the last-selected On-Key function. Press <strong>Function / Exit</strong> to return to standard play."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] How do you instantly reset the Octave shift back to default (0)?","[ ] What is the function of the Transpose button when held in combination with a keybed note?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-1-10","title":"2.1.10: 25-Key Keybed and Aftertouch Sensor","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.10: 25-Key Keybed and Aftertouch Sensor","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"1. Technical Exposition","level":4},{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II features a high-grade 25-note (2-octave, C-to-C) semi-weighted keybed equipped with dual mechanical sensor planes:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                   KEYBED MECHANICAL TOPOLOGY\n   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │ 25 Full-Size Piano Keys (C2 to C4 at Octave 0)           │\n   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n   │ Velocity Sensors  ──► Transmits Note Velocity (0–127)   │\n   │ Channel Pressure  ──► Transmits Monophonic Aftertouch   │\n   │ Silk-screen Strip ──► 25 Discrete On-Key Functions     │\n   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Velocity Sensitivity</strong>:","Scans the strike velocity of each key (0–127) to dynamically modulate Amp Envelope attack/depth and Mod Envelope depth via On-Key settings.","<strong>Monophonic Aftertouch (Channel Pressure)</strong>:","Continuous pressure strip running beneath the keybed. Applying additional downward pressure after a key has bottomed out transmits Channel Aftertouch.","Routable via On-Key to <strong>Filter Frequency</strong>, <strong>LFO 1 to Osc Pitch</strong>, or <strong>LFO 2 Speed</strong>.","<strong>Silk-Screen Function Strip</strong>:","Secondary parameters printed directly above the keys for instant physical alignment during Function mode editing."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["Strike a key lightly, then strike it forcefully; note the velocity response on patches programmed with velocity-to-filter depth.","Press a key down and apply firm, steady downward pressure. Observe aftertouch modulation engage on patches with active aftertouch routing (e.g., LFO vibrato or filter opening)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Does the Bass Station II keybed support Polyphonic Aftertouch or Channel (Monophonic) Aftertouch?","[ ] What note span (in scientific pitch notation) does the 25-key keyboard represent when Octave is set to 0?"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Section 2.2 — Rear Panel Connectivity & Routing","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Identify all nine physical interface jacks and switches across the Bass Station II rear panel.","Specify the mechanical and electrical specifications of audio output and external input jacks.","Explain the auto-sensing polarity logic of the ¼\" Sustain pedal jack.","Define the routing rules and interface limitations of the 5-Pin DIN MIDI In/Out and USB Type-B ports."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-2-1","title":"2.2.1: Power In and Mechanical Switch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The power interface comprises the 2.1 mm DC coaxial socket (<code>POWER IN</code>) and the adjacent three-position slide switch (<code>ext DC</code> / <code>OFF</code> / <code>USB</code>) detailed in Module 1."},{"type":"list","items":["Operates at 9 V DC, 500 mA, center-positive (<code>+</code>).","Ensures absolute physical isolation between mains power and USB bus power rails."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm the DC plug is firmly seated and does not wiggle.","Verify smooth mechanical detents when sliding between all three power switch positions."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-2-2","title":"2.2.2: USB Type-B Port Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear panel features a standard female <strong>USB Type-B</strong> chassis receptacle."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Class-Compliant USB 1.1 / 2.0</strong>: Compatible with modern operating systems (Windows and macOS) without requiring third-party low-level kernel drivers for basic MIDI input/output.","<strong>Bidirectional Communication</strong>: Carries real-time MIDI note events, CC/NRPN parameter automation, and high-speed bidirectional System Exclusive (SysEx) dumps for patch librarian management via Novation Components.","<strong>Power Delivery</strong>: Supplies 5 V DC operating power when the power switch is set to <code>USB</code>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Insert the square end of the USB Type-A to Type-B cable into the rear receptacle.","Confirm the cable clicks securely into place with no lateral play."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Does the USB connection transmit digitized audio from the synthesizer into the computer?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-2-3","title":"2.2.3: 5-Pin DIN MIDI In and Out Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear panel provides two standard female 5-Pin DIN sockets conforming to the official MIDI 1.0 hardware specification:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>MIDI IN</strong>: Receives standard MIDI note, pitch bend, aftertouch, program change, CC, and SysEx messages from external master keyboards, hardware sequencers, or drum machines.","<strong>MIDI OUT</strong>: Transmits all front-panel knob movements (as CC/NRPN), keyboard note strikes (with velocity and aftertouch), and SysEx data."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">               MIDI SIGNAL ISOLATION WARNING\n   [Computer USB] ──► (USB MIDI) ──► [Internal Synth Engine]\n                                                │\n   [5-Pin DIN IN] ──► (DIN MIDI) ───────────────┤\n                                                │\n   (Internal Synth Engine) ───────► [5-Pin DIN OUT]\n   \n   *IMPORTANT*: USB MIDI is NOT passed through or echoed to DIN OUT.</pre>"},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!WARNING] The Bass Station II is <strong>not a USB-to-MIDI interface (thru converter)</strong>. MIDI received at the USB Type-B port from a computer is consumed internally by the synth engine and is <strong>not forwarded to the 5-pin DIN MIDI OUT port</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect a 5-pin DIN cable from the MIDI OUT of an external sequencer to the MIDI IN of the Bass Station II.","Play a pattern on the sequencer and verify the synthesizer triggers accurately."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] If your computer sends MIDI over USB to the Bass Station II, will an external drum machine connected to the 5-pin DIN MIDI OUT receive that MIDI data?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-2-4","title":"2.2.4: Sustain Pedal Auto-Sensing Circuitry","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <code>SUSTAIN</code> jack is a standard ¼\" (6.35 mm) TS (Tip-Sleeve) mono input designed for momentary foot-switch pedals."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Auto-Polarity Sensing Routine</strong>:","The internal microprocessor executes an auto-detection routine at boot to determine pedal polarity:","<strong>Normally Open (N/O)</strong> pedals.","<strong>Normally Closed (N/C)</strong> pedals.","<strong>Calibration Protocol</strong>:","The pedal must be plugged into the <code>SUSTAIN</code> jack <strong>prior to powering on the synthesizer</strong>.","The user must <strong>not depress the pedal during power-up</strong>. If the pedal is held down while powering on, the synthesizer senses the depressed state as \"open,\" reversing pedal behavior (sustaining when released, cutting off when pressed)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Power off the synthesizer.","Plug in a standard momentary sustain pedal.","Power on the unit without touching the pedal.","Play a note, depress the pedal, and release the note; verify the note sustains until the pedal is released."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Why must you avoid holding down the sustain pedal while turning on the Bass Station II?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-2-5","title":"2.2.5: External Audio Input","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <code>EXT IN</code> socket is a standard ¼\" (6.35 mm) TS unbalanced line-level audio input."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Routing</strong>: Injects external audio signals (e.g., drum machines, other synthesizers, guitars via preamps) directly into the Mixer stage before the analogue filter.","<strong>Input Gain Adjustment</strong>: The sensitivity of the input can be adjusted digitally via the On-Key <strong>Input Gain</strong> function across a range of <strong>−10 dB to +60 dB</strong> (default = <code>0 dB</code>).","<strong>Processing</strong>: External signals pass through the Classic/Acid filter, Overdrive, Distortion, and VCA, allowing external instruments to be processed using the analogue filter and envelope envelope triggers."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect an external audio source to <code>EXT IN</code>.","In the Mixer section, select <code>Ext</code> on the shared source switch and turn the pot clockwise.","Hold a key on the keybed (or engage Arp Latch) to open the VCA; hear the external audio processed through the Bass Station II filter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What is the digital gain adjustment range for the external input jack?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-2-2-6","title":"2.2.6: Line Output, Headphone Output, and Kensington Lock","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.6: Line Output, Headphone Output, and Kensington Lock","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"1. Technical Exposition","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>LINE OUTPUT (MONO)</strong>:","A single ¼\" (6.35 mm) TS unbalanced output jack.","Carries the main monophonic analogue output signal.","<strong>HEADPHONES</strong>:","A single ¼\" (6.35 mm) TRS stereo jack.","Carries the identical monophonic synthesizer output wired in parallel to both Left (Tip) and Right (Ring) channels of stereo headphones.","<em>Operational Rule</em>: Plugging in headphones <strong>does not mute the main LINE OUTPUT</strong>. Both outputs remain simultaneously active at all times.","<strong>Kensington Security Slot</strong>:","Standard Kensington security slot located on the far right of the rear chassis for physical anti-theft cable anchoring."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["Connect an audio cable from <code>LINE OUTPUT</code> to an audio interface, and connect headphones to <code>HEADPHONES</code>.","Play a patch; verify that clear audio is present at both destinations simultaneously."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Assessment Gate A — Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before proceeding to Module 3, verify that you can execute all Gate A proficiencies without referring to diagrams:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] <strong>Top Panel Literacy</strong>: Name and physically touch all 12 control sections on the top panel from memory.","[ ] <strong>Display &amp; Values</strong>: Explain the exact meaning of the 3-character display and the dual Original Value indicator LEDs.","[ ] <strong>Rear Panel Connectivity</strong>: Locate and state the exact electrical and mechanical specifications of all 9 rear-panel connectors and switches.","[ ] <strong>Interface Isolation</strong>: Articulate why USB MIDI does not route to the 5-pin DIN MIDI Out port.","[ ] <strong>Sustain Logic</strong>: Explain how the sustain pedal auto-sensing routine functions at power-on."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
